## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Key Paradigm Shift

**Preemptive** (runtime injects exception) → **Cooperative** (code voluntarily checks for cancellation). The work function must accept and check a `CancellationToken`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw ex.InnerExceptions[0];
        }
    }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` | Task-based model; uses thread pool |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` | Timeout handled by `CancellationTokenSource` |
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.CancelAfter(timeout)` via constructor | Cooperative cancellation replaces forced abort |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | New exception type for cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No equivalent needed — cancellation is cooperative |
| `Func<T> work` | `Func<CancellationToken, T> work` | Work **must** receive and check the token |

### Caller Migration

```csharp
// BEFORE (.NET Framework)
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    () => ComputeExpensiveResult(),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));

// AFTER (.NET 8) — pass token through to work
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    ct => ComputeExpensiveResult(ct),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Critical: Work Must Cooperate

The work function **must** check the token — otherwise cancellation won't take effect until the work finishes naturally:

```csharp
// ✅ Cooperative — cancellation works
T ComputeExpensiveResult(CancellationToken token)
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return BuildResult();
}

// ❌ Non-cooperative — timeout won't interrupt this
T ComputeExpensiveResult(CancellationToken token)
{
    Thread.Sleep(60_000);  // ignores token entirely
    return BuildResult();
}

// ✅ Fix: use token-aware waiting
T ComputeExpensiveResult(CancellationToken token)
{
    token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(60_000); // wakes on cancel
    return BuildResult();
}
```

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | How to Avoid |
|---|---|
| Token accepted but never checked | Add `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` in loops and between expensive steps |
| Token not passed through call chain | Every method in the chain must forward the token |
| `CancellationTokenSource` not disposed | Always wrap in `using` — leaks timers otherwise |
| Expecting immediate stop | Cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint; design checkpoint frequency accordingly |
| Unmodifiable code (3rd-party, native) | Move to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()` — this is the only true force-stop in modern .NET |
